    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d141">

                <pb facs="0329" n="255" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg255"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Coordinator of Information</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Donovan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the President
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-05">January 5, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for the
                    President</hi></p>
                <p>No. 129. The following is a copy of a cablegram which I have just received from
                    our representative in Cairo: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“For Donovan only. Do not repeat until you hear from the President. Plans
                            are being discussed here for French North Africa, involving every phase
                            of propaganda needing printing and radio equipment in large quantities,
                            also extensive carefully-selected personnel and organization, as
                            anything British is poison to the French. This <pb facs="0330" n="256"
                                xml:id="pg256"/> effort will have to be strictly an American one, as
                            written reports on the way show British technical equipment to be very
                            scarce. Will you cable details as available, so we can inform people
                            here of our ability to meet requirements. You in Washington will know
                            immediately whether recommendations and general plans being forwarded to
                            the President are rejected or accepted.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
